  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Localized snow showers and valley rain/snow mix this morning. \n\n-  Colder temperatures, with areas of accumulating snow Friday\n   into Saturday.\n\nLow pressure spinning over northern California/western Oregon \nearly this morning is sending moisture into the Northern Rockies \nfrom the south. Model guidance brings shower activity northward \ninto north-central Idaho and along the Idaho/Montana border in \nnorthwest Montana this morning. Snow levels stay around 2,000 feet\nalong and north of I-90, and between 3,000 to 3,500 feet south of\nI-90. Localized snow accumulations of generally a trace to half \nan inch will bring generally minor impacts through this morning. \nTemperatures will climb above freezing for most valley areas by \nthis afternoon as scattered snow showers continue across mountain \nareas. \n\nA system dropping southward from Canada will set the stage for \ncolder temperatures and areas of valley snowfall Friday into \nSaturday. A trough of low pressure will slide southward along the \ndivide into north-central Montana, with a backdoor cold front \nmoving across the divide into western Montana late Friday into \nSaturday morning. This system will bring a favorable northwest \nflow pattern for snow showers along and east of US-93 corridor in \nwestern Montana. These areas have a 70-90+ percent chance for \naccumulating snow (0.1 inch or more), focused during the Friday \nlate afternoon through Saturday morning timeframe. Those traveling\nacross mountain passes along the Divide, such as Marias, \nHomestake, and Marias passes should prepare for winter driving \nconditions given colder temperatures and probabilities for 2 \ninches or more of snow at 50-60 percent. The highest snow \naccumulations will focus across areas above 5,000 feet in Glacier \nPark, the Swan, and Mission Mountains, where snow totals will \nclimb to near 4-6 inches. \n\nAn active pattern will continue Sunday into early next weekend,\nwith colder temperatures holding snow levels near valley floors.\nThese systems don't appear to bring significant snow totals, will\nbe monitored closely in the coming days.",
